@page "/exchart"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Chart

<DocsPage Product="ExChart" Title="Overview"
          Lead="Line, step and point charts of the data your application shows, drawn as SVG by Blazor. Hand ExChart a definition in your data's terms; it draws each series as what it is, and refuses rather than draws something that only looks right.">

    <DocsSection Title="A first chart">
        <p>
            A <code>ChartDefinition</code> is an axis and its series. Each series has an id, a name, a
            Series Kind and its points; hand the definition to <code>ExChart</code> with a size. Move over the
            plot to read every series at that date, click a legend entry to hide it, and drag across the plot
            to narrow the range. Under MudBlazor, <code>MudChartChrome.Default</code> draws the toolbar and the
            context menu with MudBlazor's controls, and the theme's Primary, Secondary and Tertiary colour the
            first three series.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FirstChart)" Mud="typeof(FirstChartMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/ChartData.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
        <p>
            <strong>In React.</strong> <code>@@ex-grid/chart-react</code>'s <code>ExChart</code> (published with 0.3.0)
            takes a <code>chrome</code> of two render functions, as <code>IChartChrome</code> has two parts:
            <code>toolbar</code>, handed the toolbar's commands and, while it is open, Chart Filters, and
            <code>menu</code>, handed the context menu's commands. Each is drawn through a portal where the built-in
            markup stands, and is handed a new context only when what it shows changed, so the text typed in Chart
            Filters is kept until Apply. The commands, their order, what a typed range reads as and where the menu
            opens stay the chart's: invoke a command, hand Chart Filters' <code>apply</code> the text, and the chart
            says what it could not read.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The parts of a chart">
        <p>
            These pages and the API name the parts of a chart as the lists under the figures do. The toolbar
            opens Chart Filters, shows the chart as a table and saves its files; the legend hides a series. Move
            over the plot and the tooltip lists every series of that panel at the pointer's position, while the
            guide runs down every panel. A value axis that does not reach zero keeps a break mark at its foot,
            and what the chart did to fit, such as candles merged into weeks, is said in the Notes under it.
        </p>
        <DocsFigure Name="chart-anatomy">
            Two years of an index as weekly candles with a 25-day average and the volume in a panel below, the
            pointer resting on the plot. A script draws the figure from
            <a href="figure/chart-anatomy" target="_blank">the chart on a page of its own</a>.
        </DocsFigure>
        <p>
            Under MudBlazor, the Chart Dock holds the charts made from a grid's Selection, a tab each, beside
            or below the grid and never over it. The grid's context menu makes a new chart, its series in
            columns or in rows, or adds the Selection's series to a chart already open.
        </p>
        <DocsFigure Name="chart-dock-anatomy">
            A curve history selected and charted, with the context menu open over the Selection again. A script
            draws the figure from <a href="figure/chart-dock-anatomy" target="_blank">the grid and its Chart Dock on a page of their own</a>.
        </DocsFigure>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Two ways to make a chart">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>On its own</strong>, from a definition the page builds, as above.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>From a grid's selection</strong>: the grid's context menu charts the selected cells,
                and the chart keeps following the rows it was made from. See
                <a href="exchart/grid">From the grid</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What ExChart does, and what it leaves to you">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Every series says what it is.</strong> A level is joined by a line, a rate over an
                interval is a step, an amount per period is drawn per year. A series without a Series Kind is
                refused, not drawn with a default. See <a href="exchart/series">Series and axes</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It draws only what it is given.</strong> No spline, no extrapolation, no interpolation:
                a missing value is a gap, never a zero, and a curve between quotes is your analytics', declared
                as such.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It holds no View State.</strong> The hidden series, the range and the axis come back as
                events, for the page to keep, store or hand back. See
                <a href="exchart/tooltip">Tooltip and Chart Filters</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It is read aloud.</strong> Each chart has a summary for a screen reader, and "View as
                table" shows the values it draws. Its CSV, SVG and PNG are saved on one click. See
                <a href="exchart/export">Reading and export</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Nothing per point crosses to .NET.</strong> The chart is drawn in the browser, so on Blazor
                Server a pointer moved over the plot never waits on the circuit.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A chart drawn in the browser">
        <p>
            Blazor's ExChart is drawn by the same TypeScript core as React's, as ExGrid and ExPivot are.
            <code>ExChart.Script.ExChart</code> and <code>ExChart.Script.ExSurface</code> are that chart and that
            surface, and every Example on this site draws them, through <code>@@using ExChart.Script</code>. The drawing, the tooltip, the legend, Chart Filters, View as table and the turning of a surface
            run in the browser on both hosting models, so on Blazor Server a pointer moved over the plot never crosses
            the circuit. They take a <code>ChartDefinition</code> and a <code>SurfaceDefinition</code>, and a click hands back the Row Key the point was handed in with. A
            <code>Spec</code> is read through its <code>Sources</code> in .NET, where they are: each series is asked
            for its pixels, asked again when the range narrows, and what it answers crosses to the browser as a
            definition.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreChart)" Mud="typeof(ScriptCoreChartMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/ChartData.cs"])" />
        <p>
            Its <code>Chrome</code> draws the toolbar and the context menu in Razor.
            <code>MudChartChrome.Default</code> fills both: MudBlazor's components render in your page's tree, under
            your theme, and the chart places them in the toolbar and in its menu's frame. What a control does runs in
            the browser, so a command, a range typed in Chart Filters and applied, and the axis switch never wait on
            the circuit, and a file is saved inside the click that asked for it. The downloads are a button group
            beside the other commands, and Chart Filters' ends are typed in the axis' own writing, a date as
            <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>, or chosen from a list of the categories on a category axis; what the chart cannot
            read is said under them. A Chrome of your own implements <code>ScriptSlots</code> and the
            <code>Toolbar(ChartToolbarView)</code> and <code>Menu(ChartMenuView)</code>, and marks each control with a <code>ChartScriptSlotAttributes</code> attribute: a command's id,
            the range's two ends, Apply and the axis switch. It writes no handler for them, and binds no value to the
            ends: the chart writes the range it holds into them, and reads them as Apply is pressed.
        </p>
        <p>
            Read through a Chart Source, the chart is Busy until the first answer, keeps what it drew while a narrowed
            range is asked for, and says in its place a spec its sources cannot answer.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreChartSource)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/SourcedTicks.cs"])" />
        <p>
            A .NET function cannot run in the browser. A series' or a surface's <code>Format</code> crosses as the
            text it writes each value the definition holds, so a value reads as your Format writes it; a decimal
            written <code>3.30</code> stays <code>3.30</code> in the tooltip and the summary. A
            <code>FormatCode</code> on an axis or a series crosses as itself, and the browser writes every value by
            it with the same reader as .NET's, ticks and a candle's change included. Where the chart would ask a
            function for a value the definition does not hold, it refuses the definition with a
            <code>NotSupportedException</code> that names what to change, rather than write that value some other
            way: an axis <code>Format</code>, which writes the position under the pointer, and a <code>Format</code>
            on candles, which writes their change and a merged candle's volume, each of which a
            <code>FormatCode</code> replaces; a series sampled from a .NET function of your own, which is asked a
            value at every pixel, and a <code>Format</code> on any sampled series; a <code>Format</code> on a surface axis placed by value or on a mesh's axis,
            which writes the ticks between its values, unless it writes them as plainly as a sampled surface's
            axis does; and a <code>ZFormat</code> on a mesh, whose slice reads values between its nodes, each of
            which a <code>FormatCode</code> or <code>ZFormatCode</code> replaces. Two Row
            Keys, or two values of a surface axis, that would reach the browser as one value, such as
            <code>1</code> and <code>1.0m</code>, are refused too. Charting a grid's selection and the Chart Dock
            carry over too: see <a href="exchart/grid">From the grid</a>. A candle's values are written by its
            code, or else as numbers, <code>3.3</code>, and so is every value in its CSV and under View as table,
            which keep each value as data.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreFormatCodes)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/ChartData.cs"])" />
        <p>
            A curve made by <code>Interpolate</code> or by <code>Fit</code> is the family's own, and the TypeScript core
            has its twin, held to the same cases. Such a series crosses as the points or the coefficients it was made
            from, and the browser makes the same curve again and samples it as it draws, so a spline or a fitted line,
            its Segments and its Note read as they do in .NET. A curve whose function was replaced, or a sampler that
            wraps one, is your own function, and is refused.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreCurves)" />
        <p>
            What a format code cannot say, and a curve that is neither an interpolation nor a fit, is written in your
            own script. A <code>ScriptFunction</code> (in <code>ExGrid.Data</code>) names a function exported from a JavaScript module your
            application serves, <code>ScriptFunction.Of("./js/chart-formats.js", "tenor")</code>, read against the
            page's base address as a stylesheet is. It stands where a code stands, as an axis' or a series'
            <code>FormatScript</code>, a surface axis' <code>FormatScript</code> and a surface's
            <code>ZFormatScript</code>, and where a sampler stands, in <code>ChartSeries.Sampled</code>. An axis'
            script writes what a React Consumer's axis <code>format</code> writes: the position under the pointer,
            a range's ends and a logarithmic axis' ticks, its stepped ticks staying the axis' own. The chart
            imports the module once and calls the function as it draws, on Blazor Server as on WebAssembly, with no
            round trip. Nothing is evaluated from a string, so a Content Security Policy without
            <code>unsafe-eval</code> keeps it; a module addressed by <code>data:</code> is refused. A module that does
            not load, an export that is not a function, and a function that throws or answers what its member does
            not take, text for a writer and a finite number or <code>null</code> for a sampler, are said in the chart's
            place, by name. The component drawing in .NET cannot call it, and refuses such a definition.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreScriptFunctions)" Files="@(["wwwroot/js/chart-formats.js"])" />
        <p>
            The core under the chart is the Shared Script the ExGrid package ships,
            <code>_content/ExGrid/ex-grid-script.min.js</code>, which every product over the core on the page shares,
            so a page with a grid and a chart loads it once. A Content Security Policy that lists the packages' files
            names it too.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="WebAssembly, and AOT">
        <p>
            AOT is not required. Measured on WebAssembly, ahead-of-time compilation redraws a chart about 30%
            faster, while hiding a series is no faster, and the application's download grows from 2.9 MB to
            22 MB. A chart redraws within a frame without it; turn it on only when a measurement of your own
            pages says so.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
